A||||8||fiero56||Parking Brake||||5||
Z||000000||fiero56||12-10-1999||11:39 PM||||After getting my car back on the ground etc, I reconnected the parking brake cables to the calipers. After connecting the cables, the parking brake lever only goes up one to two clicks. Before it would go about 6 or 7. Lever travel is also very limited. I have screwed around with the equalizer, but it doesn't really fix the problem. It seems like the cables shortened while they were disconnected. Is there a way I can back off the tension on them? I disconnected them again, for the time being, but when I drive the car again, I would like to have it back to normal.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Patrick||12-11-1999||04:56 AM||||<BR>Obviously, there's no way the cables are going to shorten by themselves. I would imagine that one or more cables are not seated correctly.<BR> <BR>You didn't say, but I assume that you did no other work to the brakes. Therefore, there's no reason for you to have to fiddle with the equalizer to regain the freeplay. Doing so will not correct the real problem. ||||reg||4
Z||000002||fiero56||12-11-1999||08:44 AM||||I didn't mean I thought the cables shortened on their own(I haven't lost it that much <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">. What I meant is thats what it seems like.  When the cables are hooked up they are very tight.  The parking brake lever only moves maybe a half inch up(I can only get two clicks). It's like I have the parking brake on already, and I am trying to apply it while already on. Do you guys follow? The little levers that attach to the cable on the calipers, are pulled tight away from their stops.  I did no other work on the brakes. I disconnected the brake lines, so I bled the system. Thats all that was done to them. What's wrong with it?||||reg||1
Z||000003||RZURCTN||12-11-1999||10:38 AM||||Check to make sure that the cable housing is fully seated into the the holes on the caliper and the cradle.  Also, from underneath the car, pull on the cable where they are joined to see how much travel you have from each caliper.  I had a caliper that was stuck and the parking brake wouldn't engage on that side.  So I took everything off, lubed the cables and got the caliper unstuck.   ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Raydar||12-11-1999||12:49 PM||||Just got through doing the rear brakes on mine. When I went to reconnect my cables, the left one seemed about an inch too short. Backed off the adjusting nut on the equalizer by nearly the same amount (seems like I cranked on it *forever*), in order to get it to fit. Once the brakes are worn in (haven't driven it, yet), I suspect that I'll have to tighten it some. I get 6-7 clicks, now.<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 12-11-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||fiero56||12-11-1999||07:34 PM||||The cables are connected properly, and the cable housing is hooked up right also.  Is there a bolt, or something I can loosen to create more slack?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Patrick||12-11-1999||07:50 PM||||<BR>I'd be concerned why the original slack disappeared.||||reg||4
Z||000007||fiero56||12-11-1999||08:11 PM||||I was wondering the same thing myself.||||reg||7
Z||000008||DaRkLoRD||12-11-1999||08:24 PM||||Can anyone explain to me exactly how the Fiero parking brake works? I'm looking to find out where the cables are routed, how they're connected to the rear brakes, etc.<P>I understand the basic concept, obviously, but I'm trying to figure out if it would be possible to move it to the center console, as part of a total interior change.<P>thanks!||||reg||1
